From f7c505b4324a35057e566fbdeba8b1b3cb61a864 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Polonsky <ifnotelse@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 08:39:08 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] DAOS-XXXXX dfuse: Add an option to allow idmapped mounts

Container runtimes give a container that runs in a user namespace, such as a
Kubernetes pod with "hostUsers: false", access to a host directory through an
idmapped mount of it. Since Linux 6.12 FUSE filesystems support idmapped
mounts, but only if the server sets FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP in its FUSE_INIT reply.
dfuse does not, so such a pod cannot use a dfuse mount: runc fails with
"failed to set MOUNT_ATTR_IDMAP on <path>: invalid argument".

Add --allow-idmap, which requests FUSE_CAP_ALLOW_IDMAP. The kernel only
accepts it with default_permissions, which dfuse always uses.

It is off by default because it changes what the kernel tells dfuse about the
caller of a request: for requests that do not create an inode, the uid and gid
in the header are FUSE_INVALID_UIDGID, on every mount of the filesystem and
not only on idmapped ones.

The one place where dfuse needs them for such a request is the check that it
only shares its pool and container handles with interception libraries that
run as the same user and group. When the kernel does not send them, read the
fsuid and fsgid of the calling thread from /proc, as libfuse's
fuse_req_getgroups() does for supplementary groups. The caller is blocked in
ioctl() until dfuse replies, so its thread id cannot be reused in the
meantime. The pid in the request is relative to the pid namespace of the
process that mounted the filesystem. That is dfuse, unless dfuse was given the
mounted /dev/fuse as /dev/fd/N, and then it cannot tell which namespace it is.
So a caller outside of it (pid 0), any caller when dfuse sees the /proc of
another pid namespace, and any caller of a /dev/fd/N mount cannot be
identified and do not get the handles. As for callers running as another
user, libpil4dfs then leaves their I/O to dfuse and libioil opens its own
handles. Without --allow-idmap nothing changes.

FUSE_CAP_ALLOW_IDMAP is defined by fuse libraries that support it (libfuse
commit 1f4c0608, not in a release yet). With another one, --allow-idmap has
no effect and dfuse logs that at INFO level, as when the kernel does not
support idmapped mounts.

Add an NLT test that runs libioil and libpil4dfs against dfuse started with
--allow-idmap. On Linux 6.12 and later, libpil4dfs fails it if dfuse does not
share its handles. Document the option.

Features: dfuse
